<SiteLayout title="Pension Revision for Retired Central Employees (8th CPC)" description="How pensions of retired central government employees may be revised in the 8th CPC: method, scenario table by pension, break-even factor and DR after reset." slug="retired-central-government-employee-pension-revision-8th-cpc" h1="Pension Revision for Retired Central Government Employees in the 8th CPC" lead="How existing pensions were revised last time, what the same method would give today, and what to keep ready." faq={faq} toc={toc} related={related} pageType="article" breadcrumbLabel="Pensioners: Revision">

<div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Retired central government employees are within the 8th Pay Commission's scope. In the 7th CPC every existing basic pension was multiplied by the same 2.57 factor. If the 8th follows that method, revised pension equals old pension times the factor, but DR restarts low, so only a factor above 1.63 raises the real income.</p></div>

<h2 id="scope">Who is covered</h2>
<p>The commission covers about 50 lakh central employees and about 69 lakh pensioners. That includes retired employees and family pensioners of the central government, and defence pensioners in their own scheme. State government pensioners follow their own state decisions. See <a href="/8th-pay-commission-who-is-covered/">who is covered</a>.</p>

<h2 id="method">How revision worked in the 7th CPC</h2>
<p>Existing basic pensions were multiplied by the same 2.57 factor as pay, and a minimum pension of Rs 9,000 and a maximum of Rs 1,25,000 a month were set. Dearness Relief restarted from zero on the revised pension. If the 8th CPC repeats this, revised pension equals current basic pension times the fitment factor.</p>

<h2 id="scenarios">What the same method would give today</h2>
<Figure name="pension-revision-scenarios" /><p>The table compares what a pensioner draws today (basic pension plus 63% DR) with revised basic pension at each scenario factor, before any new DR.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Basic pension today</th><th class="num">Pension + DR today</th><th class="num">At 1.92</th><th class="num">At 2.28</th><th class="num">At 2.57</th><th class="num">At 2.86</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">₹9,000 (minimum)</th><td class="num">₹14,670</td><td class="num">₹17,280</td><td class="num">₹20,520</td><td class="num">₹23,130</td><td class="num">₹25,740</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">₹20,000</th><td class="num">₹32,600</td><td class="num">₹38,400</td><td class="num">₹45,600</td><td class="num">₹51,400</td><td class="num">₹57,200</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">₹30,000</th><td class="num">₹48,900</td><td class="num">₹57,600</td><td class="num">₹68,400</td><td class="num">₹77,100</td><td class="num">₹85,800</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">₹50,000</th><td class="num">₹81,500</td><td class="num">₹96,000</td><td class="num">₹1,14,000</td><td class="num">₹1,28,500</td><td class="num">₹1,43,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The break-even point is a factor of 1.63. At that factor the revised basic pension equals today's pension plus DR, so there is no real gain. Every pension in the table above clears it from 1.92 upwards, but by a smaller margin than the factor itself suggests: a 1.92 factor is a real rise of about 18%, and a 2.28 factor about 40%.</p>

<h2 id="other">What else could change for pensioners</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Family pension</strong> is a percentage of the pension, so it rises in the same proportion.</li><li><strong>Commutation.</strong> The commuted portion is restored after 15 years today; staff bodies have asked for 12.</li><li><strong>Additional pension</strong> for those aged 80 and above may be reset with the new scale.</li><li><strong>Minimum and maximum pension</strong> will be reset along with the pay matrix.</li></ul><p>Use the <a href="/8th-pay-commission-pension-calculator/">pension calculator</a> with your own PPO figure.</p>

<h2 id="ready">What to keep ready</h2>
<ul><li>Your Pension Payment Order (PPO) and the exact basic pension shown in it.</li><li>Your bank account and pension disbursing agency details, so revised pension and arrears can be credited without delay.</li><li>Records of any past revision orders or commutation, since arrears depend on them.</li></ul>

